CONVERSATION 1: 41, two failed cycles, donor eggs suggested

Lead: I'm 41, two failed IVF cycles, and they're now suggesting donor eggs. I'm not ready for that. I feel like I'm out of time.

Sonia: Two cycles and then that conversation is a lot to absorb in a short space, and it's usually presented as the logical next step, not as a choice.

What did they tell you actually went wrong in the two cycles?

Lead: Poor response the first time, then we got three embryos the second time and none of them made it to day 5.

Sonia: That's more useful information than "it didn't work", and it points at quality, not at nothing.

Between the two cycles, was anything changed apart from the drug protocol?

Lead: No. Same everything, higher dose.

Sonia: Then the only variable that moved was stimulation, and the environment those eggs matured in was the same both times.

That's the part I work on. Not instead of the clinic but alongside it, in the months before a cycle rather than during one.

Lead: Is 41 too late for that to matter?

Sonia: No, and I won't pretend age isn't a real factor either, because it is.

What I'd say is that age is the one thing neither of us can change, so I'd want to be sure everything that can be changed has been, before you make a decision as big as donor eggs.

Lead: That's exactly what I've been trying to say and couldn't put into words.

Sonia: Then trust that instinct enough to give it three months of proper effort.

How are you doing in yourself, honestly? Two cycles takes something out of people that doesn't get talked about.

Lead: I'm exhausted. I cry a lot. I've stopped telling anyone anything.

Sonia: That's its own cost and it isn't separate from the biology.

I'm not going to promise you an outcome. Nobody honest can. I'd want to look at everything properly with you and tell you straight whether I think there's real room.

Lead: I'd rather hear that than another sales pitch.

Sonia: Then let my team look at both cycles properly on a call. Paid coaching, to be clear about it upfront.

Lead: Ok. Yes.

Sonia: Here's the link.

